WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:04.060
When it comes to genres, horror is one of
the hardest to get right.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:09.200
The smallest detail can break the spell filmmakers
rely on to suck viewers in and get them invested

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.360
in the scenes that'll fuel nightmares later.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.130
It's all about timing and balance, so it's
no surprise a lot of otherwise good scenes

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:16.289
can get the axe.

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:18.440
Sometimes, those lost scenes are awesome.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.730
Whether they give new insight into a character
or hint at a sequel, they're worth revisiting.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.560
Let's do exactly that, starting with...

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.200
A killer confrontation

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.090
The Saw series started out pretty basic.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.880
Two guys, leg chains, hacksaws… you know
the drill.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.690
Things would eventually get more complicated,
but we could usually count on Amanda staying

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.500
relatively strong... up to her violent end,
at least.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.851
As a victim turned willing accomplice, she
needed to be a little more complicated than

00:00:43.851 --> 00:00:44.910
most of the other characters.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.750
(Who were, of course, usually just torture-meat.)

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.281
Making the jump to becoming Jigsaw's pawn
is almost unimaginable, and this deleted scene

00:00:52.281 --> 00:00:54.640
from Saw 3 is a glimpse into that struggle.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.200
"Rockstar!

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:03.739
Sorry, your hair, is very rockstar."

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:07.579
It's too bad we never got to see it, because
it's particularly effective in a series that

00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:10.219
could have benefited from some relatable moments
like this.

00:01:10.219 --> 00:01:14.429
It's a flashback to an Adam who's oblivious
to what's in store for him, and a chance meeting

00:01:14.429 --> 00:01:16.359
that's about to go horribly wrong.

00:01:16.359 --> 00:01:18.929
We all know it, even if they don't.

00:01:18.929 --> 00:01:22.009
"I'll see ya there."

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:23.200
Henry's bad day

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.079
IT was one of the most terrifying movies of
2017.

00:01:26.079 --> 00:01:30.649
Pennywise gave everyone another reason to
fear clowns, but that wasn't all for the Loser's

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:33.840
Club, who also had classroom bully Henry Bowers
to deal with.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.229
They know Henry's a little unstable, and we
know it, too.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:40.219
One blink-and-you'd-miss-it deleted scene
would have helped make the full extent of

00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:42.599
his psychotic break completely clear.

00:01:42.599 --> 00:01:46.079
Henry sits in a car, watching our favorite
group of Losers head into the most terrifying

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:47.599
house we've ever seen.

00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:49.169
(Yes, even including this one.)

00:01:49.169 --> 00:01:53.280
He has company, but friends Belch and Victor
aren't feeling too talkative considering what

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.009
he's done to them… and that just really
brings it home about just what kind of bully

00:01:57.009 --> 00:02:00.140
the Loser's Club has had to deal with on a
daily basis.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.829
That's the very definition of horror.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:05.569
Say what you will about Pennywise, but at
least Henry makes your own school bully look

00:02:05.569 --> 00:02:07.329
positively pleasant.

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:09.090
Evil Dead's extra-dark details

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:14.060
The 2013 reboot of Evil Dead was as ridiculously
bloody as you'd expect, and director Fede

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.560
Alvarez once told Collider,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.690
"I know we ordered a truck [of blood] the
other day that was…

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:19.960
50,000 gallons?

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:20.960
Just for one scene."

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.140
That's a shocking amount of blood.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.620
Even though most made it on-screen for an
insanely blood-soaked finale, there were still

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.920
a few details left on the cutting room floor.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.230
In the original ending, Mia's brother, Steve,
burns down the cabin.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.570
It's a sacrificial move, as he traps himself
and their possessed friend.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:39.280
A deleted shot shows a little more of what
happens inside the cabin as it's going up

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.520
in flames, and spoiler alert: it's not good
for Steve.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.960
And, in case you've ever wondered just what
happens when the blood's stopped flowing and

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.300
the bodies are done falling, this cut scene
shows just how Mia got back to civilization.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.000
"Are you okay kiddo?"

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.500
"Help me…"

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.770
Neither shot adds anything to the story, but
it's possible that last glimpse of Steve might

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.440
have been setting up a sequel.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.300
We hope.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:08.840
"Groovy…"

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:10.460
A breathless confession

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:15.500
2016's Don't Breathe might be one of the most
tense, claustrophobic thrillers ever made,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.740
and it starts when three thieves decide to
break into the home of a blind man.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.600
It's not long before they realize they've
picked the wrong house, and it's Stephen Lang's

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.710
performance as the intended victim that's
at the heart of the film.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:29.610
Since he doesn't have much dialogue, it's
extra terrifying when he does speak — like

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.610
he does in this deleted scene.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:39.900
"My daughter was my life."

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.600
And that might be why this ended up on the
cutting room floor.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.960
It might have given him just too much to say,
going into some pretty explicit — and heartbreaking

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.350
— detail about the accident that killed
his daughter.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:55.570
While it might help us understand more about
him, the explanation is ultimately unnecessary.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.781
The scene would have thrown a wrench into
the film's pacing, but Lang's reading of it

00:03:58.781 --> 00:04:02.370
is no less mesmerizing than the rest of his
performance.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:03.569
Extra scares

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:08.630
Most deleted scenes are short and sweet, but
2002's The Ring lost an entire subplot involving

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.770
an accused murderer played by Chris Cooper,
who shot scenes to bookend the film.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:16.120
While these scenes were unfortunately never
made available to the public, a few other

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:17.590
deleted scenes have slipped out.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.779
One of the most nerve-wracking is an incredibly
creepy one in which Noah makes a horrifying

00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:26.719
discovery as he heads off to investigate the
classic sort of creepy cabin.

00:04:26.719 --> 00:04:31.840
Unfortunately for him, a small boat floating
just offshore in the lake grabs his attention.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.320
"Hey buddy!

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.260
Hey!

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.400
You okay?"

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.280
It's effective, but maybe too effective.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:46.569
The ultimately unnecessary scene — and subplot
— may have had the effect of lessening similar

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:51.050
scares at earlier and later points in the
film, and we can all agree The Ring is pretty

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.050
epic without the added weight.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:54.050
Thanks for watching!

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.389
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:56.389 --> 00:04:58.629
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

